{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1336696-finishing-reading-the-realm","id":1336696,"title":"Finishing reading The Realm","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-09-18T16:21:04-07:00","id":1336696,"title":"Finishing reading The Realm","content":"Okay, I\u2019m trying to rededicate myself to some of my lapsed projects, and that\u2019s going to start with finishing this read through The Realm. It feels a bit silly to go back to a discussion thread that has finished completely, so I\u2019ll just go with a dedicated thread for this final\u2026 44% of the book (Jesus Christ).<br /> <br /> Right, actually reading Dejis prefecture now.<br /> <br /> Something that I think is rather clever in the premise behind the prefecture being located in a rugged territory is not only how that lends itself to yielding construction materials, but is established as requiring a lot of the actual work; of <i>course</i> people living in an area with a lot of gorges and mountains would require a significant amount of infrastructure such as tunnels, bridges and decent roads, and there\u2019s a logical backstory to why somebody with a lot of pull would be situated there to place it. One might get an impression that Mnemon preferred a home base where she could more easily manage entry, and that means the area has a basis for being heavily built up in a manner that I think stands in contrast to most instances of hill lands located within larger polities, often marginalized. I also think it constitutes an understated instance of some of the anachronisms that can exist in the setting, as I\u2019d say that kind of regionally specialized and exported labour force would not be common in a lot of pre-modern economies. It\u2019s a mix of elements that nicely complements the newly enriched picture of House Mnemon, particularly with how their citizens being uncommonly well-travelled contrasts with expectations that might arise from Mnemon\u2019s haughty image.<br /> <br /> Having House Mnemon willing to do temple construction work for free or close enough seems like it would be a significant contributor to the Order\u2019s wealth, although I would imagine that far more of such work is required than the House\u2019s firms can manage, and so there would be a long waiting list for the pro bono services that might necessitate going for a more expensive bid. I like the way that Mnemon\u2019s closeness to the Order can be presented as having a militaristic element, not only in creating a zone that others would be hard put to attack (I imagine not just from concerns about the martial prowess of the monks but of the bad image it might give to another House to attack a concentration of them), but in furthering the portrayal of the Imperial Mountain as a source of unruly spirits and strange monsters. I think both of those things are a nice way to make the heartland of one of the strongest Houses (especially in the probable traditional mindset of players) into a dynamic frontier territory.<br /> <br /> For the city of Mnemon-Darjilis, I first of all like the artwork depicting it for the inclusion of a bunch of Egyptian-style pyramids among the architecture; it\u2019s a very vivid reminder that the Blessed Isle isn\u2019t all analogies to imperial China, and it feels particularly fitting the House Mnemon with how it\u2019s been described as a group that encourages its members to elaborate their faith and artistic style with foreign influences. It\u2019s also a nice realisation of the obvious image of a group of stern looking Mnemon Dynasts standing in dominance over toiling peasants, flanked by grandiose buildings, and with the Imperial Mountain dominating their own background. I don\u2019t think that I had noticed before how the House description in What Fire Has Wrought was one of the few (if not only) to not detail their home manse; I suppose it didn\u2019t feel like an absence when most of the rest referred back to where and how the founder was interred. So it\u2019s nice to have a description of Mnemon\u2019s residence; another pyramid is not what I would have expected, but I think it could be used to very effectively set a scene of somebody journeying to meet with her, of being brought up to this particularly imposing structure. I appreciate the breakdown of the components of the city\u2019s construction industry to provide a firm sense of their specific services and income streams (or precisely what scions might be placed in charge of, if coming at it from that end). I also rather like the manner in which the heavy presence of the Immaculate Order is given an economic component in the realm of purely devotional work; it feels like something that would be very inventive on the parts of the inhabitants, and I could see it corresponding with how the Order in residence likely has greatly reduced expenses (when the ruling House does most of your construction and maintenance for free, and probably makes frequent generous donatives besides, paying people to participate in rituals full time probably seems like a good pursuit when you\u2019re so flush with cash). I find it true to life of religious organisation that might not be familiar to a western background, and I think it helps to vary the Order up a bit.<br /> <br /> I particularly like what that last paragraph does for emphasizing the point that being the core of one of the most powerful Houses does not make it secure and static; that Mnemon\u2019s power invites subtle challenge by way of scheming and intrigue by its very nature, and that her strong ties with the Order are a very clear target for enemies that would want to undermine that power in a time before war breaks out. Indeed, the degree to which her security is intertwined with, even dependent upon, a religious organisation could be seen as more of a liability, especially from that line about causing some kind of embarrassment; there could be actions undertaken in the prefecture, things the House is associated with or provoked into doing, that might not repel a strictly pragmatic ally but are unacceptable for one with a moral and spiritual emphasis. I\u2019m particularly thinking of how that might be connected to instances of scions bringing back foreign heterodoxies, such as that sample armourer who designs based on inspirations from opium dreams. I could see a compelling story in Mnemon being placed in an awkward position of having to choose between House members viewed as too unorthodox and maintaining the good graces of the Immaculate Order.<br /> <br /> Hmm, how to handle the sidebars\u2026 I\u2019ll just take them as they come up, and process them as little chunks. At the outset, I\u2019ll say that it helps for the Blessed Isle\u2019s status as a setting and narrative generator to show that it can have locations with much more narrow qualities, as things like short steps in a more episodic story or quirky places to have come from (and maybe occasionally the seed of something that an enterprising player could build upon in the course of a game).<br /> <br /> For Hawk Prefecture, I\u2019m interested in that impression of a place that cultivated a reputation that could serve as the basis for official grants to give their community a specialized function, and their background of turning out serious-minded professional civil servants sounds like a fitting setting for a Dead Poets Society style story. While I\u2019m often critical of the idea of the Exalted being willing and capable of using physical force to root themselves in a situation, it\u2019s not a story that never has a place, and I find the novelty of this Tepet survivor going against the grain in teaching carpe diem to the students to be compelling in its own right, and a clever way of presenting that capacity for the Exalted to override social norms in a manner that plays off of the Realm\u2019s mores specifically. It helps that the implied trajectory of the narrative is one in which the local authorities are seeking other powers to help their cause against this unacceptable teacher.<br /> <br /> Hmm, actually, looking at Mavinyos, some of these places feel a bit less like settings with narrow applications and more like First Edition or Chronicles of Darkness style plot hooks, just ones that are the jumping off points for whole settings in their own right rather than being attached at the periphery of a larger description. For the city itself, I\u2019ve always got a soft-spot for stories of communities thriving off of salvage from coastal wrecks, so I like seeing an instance of that in the western Isle (I note that it doesn\u2019t actually attribute the wreckage to luring, as features in a lot of folklore), and the whole thing feels like it logically holds together as a den of thieves. I don\u2019t think I\u2019ve ever considered before how a lone wolf thief might upend an established crime syndicate by refusing to give a cut and being able to evade the consequences, and the intertwining of that with local politics and general rising imperial tensions feels like it could give a smaller story larger implications. It\u2019s also a clever use of limited information; it\u2019s not just that we\u2019re not given a clear indication of who the Magpie is, but that there are neighbours that the aggrieved crime lord might blame is not merely a suggested identity (or at least benefactor), but an element that might make the thief\u2019s identity ultimately irrelevant (if tensions boiling over are not the point of the targeted burglary in the first place).<br /> <br /> Winter Blossom Prefecture packs quite a bit into a small space. The brief description of the cliffs and illicit cave shrines makes for a very lovely sounding area, it\u2019s got an intriguing backstory that adds to indigenous conflicts and potential ongoing projects on the Isle as well as a basis for local idiosyncrasies, and it\u2019s got a distinct and unnerving basis for fearing the return of open war to the Isle in the form of fighting to control a major strategic pass providing openings for the dead. I especially like that sense of a probably rather old Exalt who has been around long enough to have participated in a major act of heroism in the far past and then moved on to other pursuits that jeopardize old bonds; it\u2019s not an uncommon narrative, but I\u2019m getting a sense of scale from it that plays nicely into the lifespans of the Exalted and how those can be emphasized by the length of history.<br /> <br /> All right, finish off for now with Chanos prefecture. It might seem a bit redundant to have too rugged, hilly regions in a row, but really they\u2019re quite different; Dejis is scenic inland hill territory, where Chanos is blasted, grey coastal hill territory. That\u2019s some western Ireland landscape in there, and it speaks to me. I like how Sesus takes that in to make a symbolic statement about being fires sheltered through harsh winters, and it makes for a very vivid image of maintaining lines of communication at times of the year where the climate makes being outside a real danger. I\u2019m not sure if the coast across from the Isle of Voices was ever given much detail, but it helps to emphasize the mystique of the place to have the locals distrustful of anything that washes ashore, particularly with the contrast provided by Mavinyos above in terms of the usual relationship seaside communities have with the sea\u2019s bounty (also makes the prefecture seem like an ideal locale for at least one Innsworth-esque community). Miner\u2019s Gambit sounds like a neat little local entertainment and conveyance of how people adapt to being stuck indoors (something I think Exalted has often come up with very interesting things for), although the most interesting thing about it is an example in which Dynasts are influenced by peasant culture, as well as a point of dispute across generations in a society that is usually so conforming.<br /> <br /> Chanos reminds me a lot of the depiction of cities from the Haslanti League, with the manufacturing focus and emphasis on stark economic disparity. Seeing it in these terms, I don\u2019t know, suddenly I\u2019m feeling as though I was far too harsh on that person who once insisted that Haslanti cities had a distinctly Victorian character\u2026 AdamantineRapier, that was the one. The settlement in a crescent shape with the districts progressing sequentially in affluence also reminds me of the capital of Valua from Skies of Arcadia. The city might not entirely stand out on its own characteristics, but it at least has some vivid sources of inspiration to me for its look and tone; I do think that makes its lavish and colourful Lapis Pillar Temple stand out (not least because I think that\u2019s a very distinct structure for an Immaculate temple to take). The name of the Palace of Burning Wind also gives me a nice image of its fire raining power, playing off of disturbing recent trends in major fire disasters. The Hall of Excellence Untested makes for a very stark instance of a particularly harsh primary school, but I find something strangely endearing in the idea of the Sesus legions participating in their drills, and how such a thing is a spectacle for the local commoners; those House legions have always come across as one of the more brutish elements of the imperial military, so I like this idea that at their home base there\u2019s something to give them a bit of local popularity. It\u2019s another thing that feels very Victorian industrial port town. And the spacious sewers and storm drains plus the plausible excuse for going about in concealing cloaks is a fun little touch for making the substance of the town feel like an extension of the character of the ruling House. As for the Chanos Flicker, well, I\u2019ll come back to that.<br /> <br /> I\u2019ve got to say, while it <i>says</i> that Sesus needs to contend with the uncommon experience of their home prefecture being under the authority of an appointee from another House, I don\u2019t quite feel as though the scenario is used to its fullest. Oh sure, the story of Ragara Nova tricking them into thinking that he\u2019s less capable and more pliant than he is fun enough, and I do quite like the idea of somebody handling the espionage House by turning their extreme proclivity for spying against them by presenting the fa\u00e7ade of a sieve that they trip over themselves flocking to, but it feels like a scenario that is ripe for the tension of conflicting authorities that is not followed through on by Sesus feeling like they\u2019re in control.<br /> <br /> As far as I can find, the Isle of Voices has never received very much detail compared to the sorcerous academy it houses, and the Heptagram certainly looms over the entirety of its write-up here. Yet, I find that this level of detailing gives a bit more character to the island and the institution; the Heptagram feels a bit more lively and ominous when we\u2019re told about the travails that surround it, the kinds of unnerving challenges that need to be navigated for those who traffic with it and the places that the students of the occult might venture for education, experimentation or recreation. With things like the monsters that lurk on the cliffs and coastal waters, the phantoms that surround those who approach and the prodigies hidden amidst the geography, it feels very Earthsea\u2019s Roke by way of Tanith Lee (with maybe a hint of Dagobah and Ahch-To); I like the elder tree that provides visions of one\u2019s death particularly.<br /> <br /> Not much to say about the Dragon\u2019s Blanket sidebar, other than it signposting the northern Isle as a place where bandits and the dispossessed might find refuge.<br /> <br /> Okay, so the Chanos Flicker. On the one hand, I do like the description of how the city\u2019s environment lends atmosphere to the actions of such a thief, and it plays nicely with the name to create a very tactile element to such a figure. However, it does feel a touch repetitive coming just after the Magpie; I\u2019m not saying that there\u2019s only room on the Blessed Isle for one mysterious thief defying the system and evading authorities, but it would help if they weren\u2019t right next to each other and harassing the same House. A sidebar elaborating the mystery with a selection of optional identities is decidedly not to my taste, but I\u2019ll forgive it here when the book has otherwise been so good about that kind of thing. As for those options, a probable Solar Exalted is fine, if a bit obligatory (although the description of being compelled by seeing Sesus treasures from afar elevates it a bit), and the ancient First Age servitor that can\u2019t tell the difference between its own charges and other mystical valuables is rather neat (particularly for the description of its composition). The real standout there would be Chanos Tsunbal, with the interplay between what is and is not said; the manner in which we\u2019re introduced to a figure from a fallen Great House and just hit with the bombshell that this person inexplicably finds himself in the Time of Tumult, with language even suggesting the possibility of alternate realities rather than temporal shenanigans, nicely topped off with the point that Tsunbal has unidentified powers and is being communicated with by an unknown agent. There\u2019s enough there for one to tailor an explanation, while the basic feel of the originating mystery is vivid enough that one could very well get by without one. I also like how it\u2019s wrapped in a bit of personal and political intrigue; that Tsunbal has been plopped down in an unfamiliar world and <i>could</i> go looking for an answer as to why, but his own priority is placed more on taking control of a city that he sees as his by right. Heck, even the point about promises of vengeance interplays with the matter of how he doesn\u2019t even have a sense of what it would be vengeance for, and is not immediately interested in finding out. Honestly, including this as one of the possible options kind of redeems the whole sidebar for me.<br /> <br /> And I\u2019ll take a break there. I feel as though I\u2019m getting back into the flow of things; part of why I lapsed was from a sense that a chapter of such catalogued setting information would be a bit overwhelming, but I\u2019m being reminded of how it can all serve as a very engaging read.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-09-18T16:42:01-07:00","id":1336701,"title":"","content":"I've been waiting for this. Thanks for making my day. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-09-19T15:35:03-07:00","id":1336953,"title":"","content":"Next up is Pangu Prefecture, which introduces itself well with an illustration of bustle around a canal leading out to a port. The activity, architecture and colour scheme (lots of warm browns) reminds me a lot of the town from that episode of The Last Airbender where the group ran into those pirates. I like the variety of wares and modes of peddling them on display, particularly the drover hauling a cart of lush \u201cherbs\u201d, and the junks in the background stand out nicely with their wine colour. Nice way to show that focus is shifting somewhere warmer and more verdant.<br /> <br /> I liked how Cynis was given a new emphasis on agricultural produce, so I\u2019m glad to see that depicted in this expansion on their home prefecture, both as a source of luxurious plenty and an advantage they might hold over legions that could be suddenly starved. That the home of this House would have an emphasis on artful landscaping is to be expected, but I like the reminder of how people across social strata contribute to it, with the reference to couturiers and perfumers even giving me a sense that there\u2019s a certain higher level of economic prosperity here to support such business. I\u2019m amused by the matter of how Immaculate grievances with Cynis might include making places that are too nice, drawing spirits to idleness and open revelry in a manner that needs to be reprimanded. All of that is starkly contrasted with the human cost, which I find to be conveyed with tasteful brevity and a few named locales to focus attention on.<br /> <br /> Pangu city makes for an distinct take on a Canton System, what with the Realm not restricting foreign trade in general but limiting and concentrating the traffic of the Guild specifically, and I think it\u2019s interesting that it\u2019s a monopoly that Cynis has to agitate to actively maintain. I like the description of how the wealth brought in by foreign trade results in the city being continuously built over, as I find that reminiscent of the history of Belgian city Gent, it makes for a very vivid punctuation of how such port cities can be so dynamic. And they\u2019ve still got that cosmopolitan vibe I\u2019ve been attached to with the city possessing foreign architectural and fashion styles.<br /> <br /> Yes, that impression of prosperity across the board is enhanced by the description of the city\u2019s various fine amenities. It does help to round out the portrayal of House Cynis to suggest that they\u2019re not the only ones enjoying the benefits of their value for beauty and luxury, and it\u2019s nice how their affinity for plant life is strewn throughout those attractions. I like the idea of the estuary being so heavy with boating and that it would require some skill to weave the vessels between one another, and lots of islets covered in buildings gives it a nice Adriatic look, so it all makes for a very strong backdrop for a lively drug marketplace. I think it\u2019s fun to have instances of mortal families in specialized service to the Dynasty, particularly when they compete viciously with one another as the manufacturers of anagathics do. And while I think that some of the reminders about how organized crime on the Blessed Isle falls under Dynast supervision is a bit redundant with information in the setting chapter, I enjoy the point about how a city that brings in such a mass of goods and is so steeped in business provides an opportunity for a few syndicates that are big enough to operate independently.<br /> <br /> Numinous Rolling Wave Dominion, I\u2019d been wondering about this addition to the Blessed Isle, especially its distinctly long and thin boundaries stretching from Imperial Mountain to City. Makes sense that the answer would be about maintaining a special administrative zone to monitor and preserve the conduit of Essence flowing from the Elemental Pole to the manse controlling the Sword of Creation. If the quadrants of the Blessed Isle are microcosms for the Directions of Creation, this Dominion feels like it serves as a kind of essential microcosm of the Isle itself, giving me a pristine, even Arcadian image of finely managed communities and low density of construction and the spiritually affirming beauty of the landscape. The description of how the social layers relate to one another in particular feels as though it could come from some imperial agrarian propaganda, especially that sense of the farmers selling directly to Dynastic estates. It also sounds like a very nice aspiration for outcastes in service to the Realm for their retirements and possible social advancement, although I imagine that being the prefect here has a very narrow margin of error if peasant villages can be dispossessed just for threatening the local geomancy with errant barns. This wouldn\u2019t be the first place where the Great Houses display a willingness to plunder the resources and overturn traditional policies of the Realm for short term gain, but it\u2019s especially going against the grain to jeopardize the sanctity of the Realm Defence Grid. A little description of the Imperial River Basin also appeals to me, since information about how such places accumulate populations has been a recent interest of mine, and the kind of legendary status they tend to acquire complements being placed alongside Numinous Rolling Waves literal power nicely.<br /> <br /> Now, this Valley of the Ancients\u2026 the Egyptian Valley of Kings is so iconic that it makes a lot of waves in popular culture, and it wouldn\u2019t even be the first time it pops up in Exalted since it\u2019s probably a major influence of the Scavenger Lands. And yet, this is more than just placing such a thing in the heart of the Realm, it\u2019s something to capture some of the interesting contradictions inherent to the relationship between the Scarlet Dynasty and the First Age. Even if they\u2019re appropriating them (although I\u2019d suspect more than a few Dynasts have a better idea of the identities of the interred), it still speaks to how the artistic and technical achievements of the ancient Solars can remain a source of awe and fascination to the modern Dragon Blooded. At the same time, a willingness to build their homes there seems more than just a wish to reside amid the monuments to bygone glory, but as another symbolic gesture of dominance and usurpation. And yet, they\u2019re still kind of subjecting themselves to the memory of the Solars, not just in things like admiration for constructions from their ethos and digging for the products of their artifice, but the degree to which retirement here takes over their lives. On that note, I\u2019m quite tickled by the notion of this divine hero retirement community that really ultimately serves as a kind of gilded cage for its inhabitants; the idea that a place really built up to seek residence in having an ulterior motive is nothing new, but I think it plays off nicely with the personality of the Scarlet Empress to make the catch not something overtly sinister but just the fact that the cost of living here is prohibitively high, and you\u2019ve got some of the worst neighbours. It hits a lot of the same notes to me as that Lunar Shangri-la kind of place, where it\u2019s an isolated community but isn\u2019t static and boring, with things like changes to one house requiring adjustments to all of them, tomb delving, handling some of the strongest hungry ghosts and general small town high society intrigue. I want to think that murder has some huge implications here, because it seems to me that rendering any one dwelling uninhabited has dire implications for everybody, since adjustments likely have to be made across the board even if a building is demolished entirely (which is no small cost on its own). Heh, the Festival of Remembrance even more obviously makes that point about how they\u2019re inadvertently tying themselves ever deeper into a kind of symbolic service to the Celestial Exalted; I never would have expected any part of the Realm to engage in ritual of celebrating and venerating the Solars and Lunars, with pride of place in taking up their roles in narrative, even if they do save pride by filing the serial numbers off, and it\u2019s all delightful. The illustration is a little bit sparse, but that in itself kind of makes the point about what life in the valley is like, and the manses themselves are very attractive in their little models. And for the sidebar on the Ancients, this is a case where I don\u2019t mind it being direct about them actually being fallen Solars (although I\u2019m often a little bit iffy about books directly addressing readers in a colloquial manner), but I like the descriptions of the little tall tales that have accumulated around some Solars, that might invite players to figure out what the truth behind those feats and how they were achieved was; I think that\u2019s a very good way to handle portrayal of Celestial and other Exalted as figures of ancient and somewhat embellished legend, allowing such stories to not limit themselves to strict capabilities from the mechanics and providing some encouragement to come up with novel powers or applications of the familiar ones.<br /> <br /> One last thing I\u2019ll say is that I don\u2019t think it would have been amiss to have been given a few profiles for some of the local authorities... although I suppose that such has been absent thus far anyway.<br /> <br /> Hmm, not too much, but I\u2019ll leave it there for the moment; if the Scarlet Prefecture is next, I want to give it my full attention. For now I\u2019ll just say that I\u2019m liking this approach of starting at the centre and spiralling around, I think it should make for easy referencing in future, and conveys a nice sense of following a gradually transitioning vignette around the Blessed Isle.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-09-24T10:28:01-07:00","id":1337658,"title":"","content":"Rather busy weekend.<br /> <br /> <br /> The Scarlet Prefecture has definitely gotten a bit of a revamp, although I find it to be in an understated way that\u2019s a bit hard to articulate. First and Second Edition each portrayed it a bit differently between them, but the impression I get here is that it\u2019s being rendered a bit more naturally formed and densely populated, less of the pristine backyard for Dynasts living near the Imperial City. Indeed, with the description of settlements having accumulated around Exalted estates and service industries placed along the roads, it gives me an impression of being a model of Edo period Japan nestling in the heart of the Blessed Isle, and I also like how it calls back to the origin story of the Scarlet Empire. I recall times in the past where there\u2019s been a comparison between Nexus and the Imperial City in which they possessed the same wealth but the latter had it much more static and concentrated at the top, but I think it makes a more inviting picture to give the place tension arising from how it provides opportunities for commoners to make their fortune.<br /> <br /> That also plays off nicely with the new life breathed into the patricians here, building off of their increased prominence and independence in the ministries and that plot hook about wanting to establish more autonomy for themselves in What Fire Has Wrought (was it anywhere besides Kingfisher Swift\u2019s chapter fiction, I can\u2019t recall). I can see a lot of narrative potential in the point about how the perceived need to band together is grating against a long history of internecine conflict, especially for Dynast players; a character could probably make a name for themselves by securing a staff of skilled administrators by effectively pressing on bad blood to prevent them from banding together, an area that a lot of House elites might not be so focused on. Alternatively, an outside might secure a major power bloc on the Isle for helping to smooth over differences and win over clans with a more longstanding loyalty to a Great House to ensure that the patricians of the Imperial City are a force to be reckoned with. In any case, I like the inventiveness of how they\u2019re smuggling their own troops into their estates.<br /> <br /> Nice to see a dedicated description of wilderness reserves for upper class hunting, that kind of thing stands out to me as a way in which the wealthy can shape landscapes throughout history. It seems as though the Scarlet Prefecture is big enough to allow that kind of thing without many farmers feeling as though they\u2019re being crowded out, but holding dangerous beasts for the sake of providing challenging hunts for the Exalted might be a source of contention. There are times when I\u2019ve felt as though the Scarlet Empress is depicted as hardly ever leaving her palace, so I\u2019m happy to see her given a personal reserve, and it lends a bit more to her sorceress character for it to be stocked with some particularly strange sounding entities (I guess that keeping such things so close to the capital is a privilege of being the absolute monarch). It\u2019s come up before without me mentioning it, but with three of them here I\u2019ll say that I\u2019ve been liking these manses being given distinct names as a way of conveying powers and unique characteristics. That they\u2019re prominent in this prefecture lends a bit to me for that idea of their focus being on something other than the patricians, as well as a possible asset that they might make use of to safeguard their interests from the Great Houses if they can snake them out via friendly outcastes, even if they obviously have utility as a means of the Dynasts overwhelming the prefecture or turning it into one of the most fiercely contested battlegrounds. Between this and Numinous Rolling Waves, it would appear that they\u2019re laying down an idea that Mnemon, Ragara and Sesus are prominent in the manse game, I wonder if that might be developed in future. In any case, I like this being yet another front in which young new House V\u2019neef has to figure out how to manage its assets on the fly.<br /> <br /> Aaaand we at least get one prefect with a return of good old Mnemon Bata. I\u2019ve always had a fondness for him, or at least for interesting things he illustrated about his mother and her relationship with unExalted Dynasts. Mnemon has a bit more going for her now, but he\u2019s still got that fun element from having gone from possibly the most boring job in the empire to somebody having fun making his relatives beg him to do things he was already on board for. I even see shades of Mnemon\u2019s sentiment that her son is respectable but might need to be killed if he keeps pushing his look in the line about how he doesn\u2019t know the risks he\u2019s taking, although it\u2019s probably just about how he\u2019s highly placed in what is going to be hotly contested territory.<br /> <br /> Now, the Imperial City itself. First of all there\u2019s the architectural quality, which I think consolidates a number of things I\u2019ve liked in previous descriptions of the city. For instance, I\u2019ve often thought that the First Edition core gave it one of the strongest senses of being constructed from unusual materials at the behest of demigods, and the whole premise that they need to continuously build upwards and over streets is both a fun stylized rendering of how crowded cities have historically been as well as a logical extrapolation of how a city might be confined by something such as dependence on a mystical resource or location. I think the latter is a bit less extreme here, makes the place feel a bit more navigable (more room for parades and festivals at least), and the latter has been expanded considerably from just buildings of marble and obsidian with things like containment of First Age devices and the public lighting. I think that the Imperial City has often captured the sense of being the centre of the strongest empire in the world in its scope but been somewhat bare in its monumental features, and that\u2019s more than made up for here with things like huge statues flanking the entry gates. The thing I really love is the addition of stolen treasures and monuments from the corners of the empire and general conquest, to really emphasize the kind of power that they\u2019ve abused and give the place some authoritarian flair; I like the mosaic made from ground up thrones particularly. I think it\u2019s clever how the Palace of the Deliberative is made more multicoloured to reflect the greater variety provided for the garb of its members (and I\u2019m glad how the structure is tied back to House Mnemon specifically), while the range of services surrounding them strike me as useful as a means of clandestinely approaching the place or supplies that can be manipulated around it. I get why the detail of the Deliberative having once been purged en masse by the Scarlet Empress was removed, but it was something I was fond of, so I appreciate at least having the memory of it preserved in the garrison at the Strix Tower standing as a tacit threat by her against the senators; it allows for the possibility that the reason there\u2019s never been such a massacre is that no Deliberative has ever felt secure enough to chance their arm with repeatedly overturning the veto.<br /> <br /> I suppose that if the prior sections gave the City some more open spaces for monument, ceremony and the housing of the elite, it needs to be balanced out by making the commoner sections sound more cramped and segregated than ever. This is something that actually does bring me back to the idea that the Imperial City is a concentration of huge wealth that might have the greatest disparity of it in Creation; combined with its origins as a community that, if not exactly planned, sprung up very quickly and precisely, it gives me this image of a capital in which even being a centre of finance and administration is a secondary concern, that its first purpose is to be the heart of the Realm\u2019s immensely powerful ruling class, residing in the uttermost heights of luxury, and drawing in the incredible amount of human labour required to sustain that. Having watched some things about Diseyland recently, I like the image of how the social divide is literally preserved by the architects in service to the Scarlet Dynasty (and now more overtly and clearly significant patricians) by obscuring partitions. Yet it also conveys that the Imperial City is a lot more than the function that the Empress and Exalted would want of it, not just with the detailed criminal element but the matter of it being multi-ethnic, which always makes for a lively and chaotic space in which people pointedly assert their identities in ways that allow a variety of mixtures and clashes. It feels like the powerful needed to draw large numbers of people from every direction to ensure they were properly serviced, and in so doing stumbled upon an appropriately messy and organic city. That makes for a very nice setup for the point that the rising tensions in the Realm are not something going unnoticed by the inhabitants of the capital, and how it\u2019s making them very antsy, particularly with the addition of a more authoritarian threat in the Black Helms encouraging people to inform on one another. I think the Imperial City in the past has been a little bit bland, but this focus makes it feel like much more of a powder keg that could explode beyond the capacity of even the Scarlet Dynasty, let alone the more locally intriguing patrician clans, to predict or contain, and that reminder that there\u2019s energy in the lower classes is appreciated by me. The artwork depicting a scene of the city\u2019s outer wall is fine looking, and I like the sense of scale in things like the dusk lighting, but doesn\u2019t quite catch my eye.<br /> <br /> Let\u2019s see, just a moment for the sidebar on Gri-Fel\u2026 I think the God of the Imperial City has always been a mostly good one (even having moved on from the misunderstanding of both how gods worked and the origins of the Imperial City; I can\u2019t actually recall off-hand if he was written up in First Edition), and I think this entry captures the core of what is interesting about the character\u2019s humble origins and peculiar dedication to Immaculate beliefs while trimming a bit of the mercenary attitude that I thought brought him down a little and a sense of being even more strongly focused on the Imperial City (where a few elements before made him feel a little bit too much like an additional god of the Realm). I think those elements complement each other well, where he perceives his domain as the very heart of the Perfected Hierarchy, and I think the more expansive take on the city could provide him with some interesting challenges in the event that he takes a more interventionist stance.<br /> <br /> And what of the heart of the heart of the Realm? I\u2019ve been very curious about the Imperial Palace with the adjustment to the Great Houses to make them more decentralized, seeing as how the Second Edition write-up felt like it was a Forbidden City that consisted almost entirely of wings to be home bases for all of the Great Houses but Nellens, which don\u2019t get me wrong, I thought was an interesting idea for the time, just something that probably wouldn\u2019t be the case now. It immediately starts off with something new in the form of the palace complex being divided from the rest of the city by an ornate and heavily patrolled wall, where the Second Edition version pointedly had no such thing; I suppose that when Lunars are played up as a more dedicated threat, you can\u2019t really afford to make gestures of indifference to dangers. That also goes for the more direct details of the sorcerous security incorporated into the palace, which seem visible enough to also give an ominous air to those visiting legitimately, and make it more overtly feel like the house of a sorceress; I particularly like the reference to defences against the ghosts of those killed by the Empress\u2019 policies (which reads to me more as instances were such things were mere side effects of larger institutions, rather than when her purpose was to have many killed). I\u2019m also glad to get a clarification of the extent of those defined as sharing the blood of the Empress for purposes of requiring a bearer of the Imperial Seal to permit one to enter; all Dynasts is a bit broader than I would have expected, but then the idea that such an impermeable safeguard is employed, with so many delegated to screen entrants, itself conveys an image of the palace being a focus of much business.<br /> <br /> And as well there should be such elaborate defence, because the palace within feels much more lively and interesting. It covers similar ground to what I said before about the Empress feeling somewhat isolated at times; Second Edition\u2019s Scarlet Pavilion was sizable to be sure, but between it seeming as though within her own palace there was one building among hundreds that was actually inhabited by her and the lack of description for its features, it made the Imperial Palace feel more like a home base for the Scarlet Dynasty than the residence of the most powerful monarch in the world. That felt like a bit of a missed opportunity, since their residences are so often an extremely significant means by which monarchs project the image of their power to the masses of their subjects, to cultivate the idea that this is more than just another person but the very embodiment of the state, as well as a form of definition for what the ideals and identity of that state are, and that\u2019s something that ought to be shown to the reader by getting a sense of what architectural and artistic propaganda one is being bombarded with. Not to mention the idea that having such power ought to (or is at least expected to) come with a lot of creature comforts. That and they\u2019re often just fun things to read about. I think that potential is much more effectively being capitalized on here by the descriptions of the various official buildings, landscape ornamentation, and especially the buildings most closely associated with the Empress herself. I get why artwork depicting the Scarlet Empress tends to rely on the shorthand of making her look fairly similar in all instances for instant recognisability, but I particularly like the point of how somebody that has been reigning continuously for almost eight hundred years would accumulate such a variety of regalia that it all gets stored in a place designated the Hall of Ten Thousand Seals (I like that parasols are among her regalia especially), and the Jinmai Tower housing numerous menageries divided by region carries interesting implications about the image she wanted to project, and possibly even her personal interests. The same goes for the descriptions of residences set aside for others who were personally close to her, which I find to reinforce the sense I got before that her forming intimate relations was given more emphasis in a way that I found a bit more humanising; I like that her having had such is prevented from falling away as a distant abstract by giving her a still living ex-husband and actually referencing a mortal child for once. I also like her having possessed an advisor who is not a native of the Realm or Sidereal Exalt (at least overtly; it\u2019s not inconceivable that one might employ a mortal astrologer even while actual Star Chosen are on hand). All that\u2019s missing there is a personal jester. The description of how official would conduct themselves as they moved about the palace, that calm, graceful marching to cultivate a serene sense of timelessness, is both a nice insight into the underlying philosophy of the palace and a good area to place a reference to the ritualistic practice of monarchical residence (and even general government) found in places such as imperial China and Japan. And while the place is clearly not unreservedly open to the public, the references to museums, libraries and theaters and how (at least some ranks of) peasants might enter gives me a sense that there would be a form of controlled entry to allow people to bear witness to that grandeur and consume the official histories, so that it could be carried back out into the greater Realm (even while I know that any one of those three things can be subjects for much smaller audiences; the palace just seems big enough to include it). The palace is finalised in feeling like the concentrated centre of the city and empire as a whole when it\u2019s described as a place where the formerly orderly and dutiful conduct has given way to backroom intrigue for individual advancement or drawing the factional battle lines; for anybody playing a Dragon Blooded game including characters focused on those endeavours, it makes the Imperial Palace seem like the ultimate arena in which to ply their skills and reap some of the grandest rewards.<br /> <br /> And last of all is the paragraph and accompanying sidebar about the Imperial Manse. Its overall literal and narrative position hasn\u2019t changed, and I find that it doesn\u2019t belabour the point about them. I notice that the garden in which the Manse resides has been changed from an open field of red flowers to a more enclosed space of tall trees, and I wonder if that holds any deeper significance than just a change of scenery; I also take the implication that, while squat, the Manse has been made somewhat larger and more imposing than its previous description of standing one storey off the ground. The most important thing I take from this section are the little descriptions for the rumour and hearsay of what mysteries and oddities reside within, more even for what they say about the world view of the inhabitants of the Realm than what they could be used for in stories about the Imperial Manse itself.<br /> <br /> I\u2019ve not summarized individual entries for this chapter yet, but the Scarlet Prefecture is a rather prominent element across Editions (after all, Compass: Blessed Isle devoted an entire chapter to it), so I want to say that I find this writing to have really made it pop in ways that it didn\u2019t manage before. The new life injected into the Imperial Palace especially.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-09-27T16:17:45-07:00","id":1338204,"title":"","content":"Heh, my initial thought was that Sdoia prefecture was something entirely new, but I see that it was an addition for Compass: Blessed Isle that I just would have overlooked or forgotten before. Comparing the entries now, I can see that they\u2019ve gone and consolidated this prefecture with the features of neighbours that it used to be described as holding in contempt. So it\u2019s now characterized by housing both resort features for Dynasts who want to retire somewhere that is still within range of the Imperial City, and the home port of the Wood Fleet, things that feel a bit like they complement each other; the military air making the resort more threatening, the resort making the naval base a bit more relaxed. It\u2019s particularly good to give us an example of an idiom for the Dynasty (appropriately obnoxious and privileged). The city of Sdoia has some good characteristics with its economic disparities and lighthouse fortresses and common decoration with flags; the way that the fleet prevents there from being room for any commercial shipping reminds me of a time Grabowski made a point about how galleys take up a lot of space, and it\u2019s a distinctive quality for a port town. But the important thing is providing a focus on plot going forwards, and it\u2019s good to make this another one that might be what sets the actual war in motion. The reference to Peleps being concerned with Sesus infiltration of the navy in What Fire Has Wrought caught my interest, but there wasn\u2019t exactly room there to develop it, so I\u2019m happy to see it given focus here as the basis for some tense spy games. The idea of somebody from House Sesus being prefect of somewhere very important to Peleps raises some intriguing questions about who was behind that appointment; if Tudara dates back to the time of the Empress, I\u2019d wonder if definitively uncovering her as the source of sabotage of the Wood Fleet (the fleet charged with patrolling the waters bordering Lookshy, don\u2019t forget) would cause greater ire in Peleps for the House, the Deliberative, or even the Empress herself. Mind, having appointments come from the Deliberative now feels like it provides an effective counterweight for the greater political significance of prefectural appointments; the Empress can do something like keep Peleps on their toes by placing a major base under the jurisdiction of a rival, and if that gets uncovered she can just shift the blame to manoeuvring in the Deliberative.<br /> <br /> Some more short entries, starting with one reduced down to the scope of a single household, a scale for such plot hooks that I was not expecting. Heh, it\u2019s timely that I should come across these Relins of Raiton\u2019s Perch so soon after having had an amusing conversation with my brother about the dynamics of an aristocratic family of faded glory found in the Downton Abbey film. I\u2019m also fond of stories about such folks living in an isolated state with their own weird servants, your Eol the Dark Elf, your nameless castle of House Baelish at the stormy end of the smallest Finger, even your Gormenghast, and there\u2019s a nice Exalted twist in things like some proud forgotten Shogunate ancestry and command of an old sorcery to maintain a modicum of comfortable living; the idea of a working to gather sunlight to improve harvests sounds very much like one of the more interesting and esoteric ideas that Vance once floated (although technically inverted, as that one was about extracting the sunlight from existing crops), grounded in a bit of practical application. Something that had also been on my mind was the notion of wildcards popping out of the probabilities of Terrestrial Exaltation, so I like this instance of a group improbably producing multiple Dragon Blooded in short order to very suddenly give them an infusion of power in a fragile period. I especially like the presentation of a dark horse in the Realm\u2019s tumults that has ambitions and maybe no small amount of capability to make an independent domain of their own amidst the struggles of the Great Houses, particularly if they\u2019ve spent a long time resenting and coveting richer neighbours that they might conquer; I\u2019ve got this most compelling image of Vane Prefecture being just out of the way enough, and so many distractions elsewhere, that the Relins have breathing room enough to seize initial power solely through applying the force of their few Exalted and their enhanced sorcerous working, rather than conventional military action. Such a coup might be a tricky proposition even for powerful Exalted in other times and places, but I feel as though a sufficient show of force against people used to security at a time when they\u2019re surrounded by uncertainty and the usual defenders are far removed could very quickly break resolve.<br /> <br /> Tears-of-the-Lotus makes for another nice example of a creepy waterside village, and I like how the more ominous and threatening House Iselsi is used as the basis for a ghost story, especially directed at poor beleaguered House Nellens. It\u2019s interesting how it takes the character of the fallen House while not being directly concerned with or connected to the Vendetta, giving a nice sense of some surviving scope in a clan of Exalted even after their fortunes take a downturn, and gives them a stronger sense of history and roots, particularly in terms of them having left the Blessed Isle with a legacy that their opponents might not shake off so easily. I can see potential for this in plotlines where Iselsi\u2019s Vendetta becomes more apparent to the rest of the Dynasty over time, and finding evidence of things that the House left behind becomes a local equivalent to unearthing an unexploded bomb. I like the inclusion of the distinct tulou architecture and all. And Ramidel\u2019s Seat is so eye-catching on the map that I\u2019m glad it gets a bit of attention, and making it the site of a major transformative assault by the Lunar Exalted is a good way to give the Silver Pact\u2019s hostility some real impact on the Realm. I think it\u2019s especially good to make it a comparatively recent victory, a fresh wound that those affected are still trying to navigate the aftermath of and a sense that the violence of the Lunars is an ongoing concern. My one reservation is with the sense of scale; I feel as though the description of the island\u2019s geography and economic functions apply to something the size of a Balearic or Canary island, but on the map we\u2019ve got something that is larger than Great Britain.<br /> <br /> With those points about House Iselsi in mind, I\u2019m rather excited to see what happens with Incas Prefecture now. The land itself seems to be the generally pleasant that characterises the eastern Blessed Isle, albeit a bit lower topographically than the previous ones have been; picturing those cash crops hemmed in by mountains makes for an attractive image. I like being given an example of a place that soldiers might want to retire to. And the Radiant Synod is an interesting approach to depicting a local spirit court, the way that they\u2019re just notables of the area that have characteristics that make them a draw for other people like suspicious Immaculates or Solars who might be looking for an inroad to the Isle, but in their own right are just there. I like getting something like that occasionally, a power that doesn\u2019t exactly have their own agenda but can be a focus for the ambitions of others; it\u2019s like, some gods are just hanging out at ease, but they\u2019re still gods and the right person might find a cause around them.<br /> <br /> I\u2019ve often thought of centres of textile production as one of those things that is historically rather economically integral but kind of unsexy, and so seems to often get overlooked in things like historical analysis or fiction (and things like fantasy that draw upon them). So Sion looks good as something that represents that and still makes it sexy by presenting the place as a centre of couture fashion to feed the appetites of the Scarlet Dynasty\u2026 which turns out to be mostly what it was in prior Editions (another thing I had overlooked in the past), so props to the writers back then for having a good idea that could come through mostly intact here. The main difference seems to be taking the original write-up for the city and electrifying it; not just home to artisans noted for beautiful clothing, but full on eccentric couturiers, not just a yearly festival for designers to gather and contracts be made but its own rendering of Paris Fashion Week by way of the Champagne Fairs. I find it to be reminiscent of Djelis Prefecture in its employment of somewhat anachronistic elements to good effect (I\u2019m pretty sure one wouldn\u2019t expect to get this kind of industry in a world that lacked automated cloth manufacture), and I imagine that a centre of high fashion competition and intrigue can make for an effective setting for a different kind of Dynast game. Not necessarily a lower stakes one, I feel as though it\u2019s the kind of place that can invite one to really explore the depths of significance to social standing and conspicuous consumption in a group like the Scarlet Dynasty.<br /> <br /> Oh yeah, the Immaculate Order chapter never did actually describe the Palace Sublime itself, did it? Same basic layout as the description in Second Edition (as far as I can tell the first time the headquarters of the Immaculate Order was described), the two very tall towers standing astride some squat, plain buildings, although this time we\u2019re actually told a lot more about the function of those towers beyond being the residence/meeting area of the Mouth of Peace and support staff; the same one as herself has the record halls and lodgings for guests and esteemed permanent residents, the other being the home of their libraries and archives for spiritual and academic texts and records of Order business, plus scriptoriums. With administrative functions distributed to the towers and the Palace no longer being the training area for the entire Order, the central fort gets its own overhaul as the dedicated training facility for Dynastic postulants, and there\u2019s your obligatory vaults of treasure and forbidden power secreted beneath. This is the kind of description of such a building that I like, a firm, clear sense of its practical functions without being too obsessed with the precise geography of it. Unlike the Imperial Palace, this one gets to keep its posturing of having no defensive walls, although I get the impression that there are fewer Lunars and the like that are willing to chance their arm going for a spot where they\u2019re likely to encounter multiple Immaculate martial artists (that and how the Empress might need a bit more defending against people that are technically allowed to enter than the Order does). As with other aspects of the Order, I\u2019m glad to see references to Sidereal Exalted in residence and running the show removed, even if we can still infer that Chejop can be expected to be around at times.<br /> <br /> And last the city of Noble is\u2026 also basically what it always was, a military town facing hard times in the current climate as legions are withdrawn more deeply to the interior. It looks to me like they place a greater emphasis on the economy having been dedicated to servicing military units in transit specifically, and I get the impression that it\u2019s dropping the origin of having been founded at the start of the reign of the Empress in the interests of lengthening the history of the Realm. I had an initial impulse of being critical of the fact that it doesn\u2019t have any associated forwards-looking plot hooks, but reflecting on it more I do think there\u2019s a place for giving a firmer sense of the uncertain times for the Blessed Isle for simply presenting a community that modern concerns has left hopeless and dying.<br /> <br /> Huh, didn\u2019t really turn out to be any traps left behind by Iselsi. I guess that can be part of what one gets when a House is downfallen, a lot of its influence might be excised right out, and I suppose there\u2019s a point that Iselsi\u2019s deliberately laid traps are something they want to set where people might not be as closely looking for them as in their traditional homeland. Still, the sidebar on the province of the Luo-Han Plains gives an impression that the neighbouring region is still one that they\u2019re familiar enough with to find useful as a means of communicating discretely, and I could always take it that, while Noble and Sion were valuable as sources of funding, their particular industries might have drawn a bit too much attention to be viable as staging grounds for covert operations, in comparison to out of the way shrines. As for the Plains as a whole, I enjoy the descriptions of how being in such close proximity to the heart of the Immaculate Order and a typical starting run for fresh monks has its benefits and drawbacks for the inhabitants in the form of being sure that both officials and peasantry will be scrutinised closely for proper conduct, and it\u2019s a nice touch that a disproportionately large number of monks elect to be interred there.<br /> <br /> Let\u2019s see\u2026 yeah I can fit in one more, the next prefecture is very short.<br /> <br /> Ah good, another entirely new addition in the form of Ragara\u2019s home Corin Prefecture. What Fire Has Wrought would have already told me about it being a rugged landscape from which the House extracts mineral wealth, but here it\u2019s further characterised as a place covered in overt expressions of the House\u2019s wealth in the form of ostentatious mansions and monuments; I find it to be sufficiently differentiated from Pangu by the focus less on aesthetic beauty than on scale, complexity and the value of the construction materials, and it\u2019s a delightful bit of dramatic irony that putting so many funds into such an endeavour means that a permanent household of members of Mnemon of all families resides there. I have to imagine that they have a frosty relationship with the matriarch, even if they\u2019re being paid. Something I like in particular about this section is in its pointing out how this stuff isn\u2019t just indulgent or showing off, but actually serves as a means of converting wealth in the form of accounts and financial instruments into something a bit more stable and tangible, which I think goes well with the point that local patricians can buy loyalties not with hard currency but the offer of being able to marry into the area. It\u2019s an interesting point about how Ragara\u2019s wealth is more than just actual coinage or even the debt they can leverage over clients.<br /> <br /> I also find the House to strike an interesting balance in its relationship with the local commoners; that they\u2019re not exactly loved or have any love themselves, but at least value enough stability to make sure that things run without overt cause for objection. I like seeing the House\u2019s Aspect in this policy, and it makes for a very strong contrast with House Mnemon, how their Earth Aspect in Djelis comes from promoting spirituality and investing the common people in the value of that traditionalism, where Ragara just wants things to be efficient enough that they remain static. I also like how, for all of this, there remains an underlying tension owing to them being unable to quite shake the legacy of their matriarch being basically the worst human; I\u2019m kind of imagining a scenario in which Ragara actually does finally die, not of anything dramatic or actionable, his life just finally gives out, and an attempt to hold a public funeral for him ends up provoking a really massive riot. Not because of something specific, just because there can\u2019t help but being this massive release of backed up energy when there\u2019s a really old history of bad blood that was quietly backed up over such a long period of lacking the catharsis of him ever having actually died.<br /> <br /> The What Fire Has Wrought write-up also had this really nice metaphor about the Imperial Mountain rising proudly but having dark secrets beneath its roots that made for a nice image of the House itself, and I like seeing that also reflected in their homeland in the form of a lot of overgrown ghost towns and exhausted, closed off mines that not only accumulate a lot of vagabonds and people on the margins (I don\u2019t think mines have ever been something historically poached as such, but poachers is the image I\u2019m getting of how the House would treat prospectors who try to eke out some scraps that aren\u2019t worth the trouble to Dynasts but could make a peasant comfortably well-off), but how they try to dig out any lost magics that might be lurking beneath the surface. Recovery in the aftermath of an earthquake is an unexpected inciting incident for them, and I like how their concern isn\u2019t just about anything powerful that might have been opened up, but needing to figure out how to regain access to productive mines that have been buried.<br /> <br /> Riven Quay would be all well and good as an industrial centre, but I really love how it marries that to the point of being a brownwater port for the sake of gathering the transported raw minerals to give the city a distinct character with spiritual cleansing imagery as a necessity of life amid all of the smog and smells, while also being another avenue for the expression of wealth in terms of things like what you spend in shielding your home from the general unpleasantness and going about smelling of roses. It\u2019s like\u2026 Shinto cleansing practices blended with Roman baths blended with London\u2019s concerns with the purity of the Thames, it\u2019s very clever. It also feels like it would have a plausible basis for a higher focus on heresy than the other overcrowded cities when its issues create greater pervasive health detriments, even before getting to the point of how Ragara lets it slide so that Immaculates are more occupied with suppressing god worshippers than realise that the ruling House flirts with blasphemy. Ragara might not have been my favourite new take on a House (and it was still pretty good), but I think this is my favourite take thus far on how a House relates to its home prefecture and city.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-01T19:51:08-07:00","id":1338917,"title":"","content":"Ha! I\u2019d been thinking for the last few entries that it was getting time to move away from the coasts, so I\u2019m happy to see that the next entry is specifically setting itself apart from those zones. Where better to take a range inland than Juche prefecture, previously depicted as the heart of the Blessed Isle\u2019s mining, a major manufacturing centre, the only home afforded to House Nellens and a meeting zone with\u2026 are those the fucking Mountain Folk?! My that is\u2026 that is quite the design departure, it is going to take a while to process it. I am digging the way that the castes are distinguished by increasing levels of ornateness to their crystalline bodies, though, and the provisionally named Artisan\u2019s crown-like head crest, and it\u2019s fun seeing Realm peasants reacting to them with a certain look of, how do I put it\u2026 maybe a kind of perturbed but familiar wariness, directing attention but not particularly surprised by such an odd sight. Honestly, the most uncanny thing about the picture might just be the sight of them using horses. Let me just see\u2026 yes, what does this sidebar have to say? It seems to be mostly keeping the cards close to its chest for the moment, just reiterating the basic information about residing beneath the surface, being excellent crafters, provide a tribute of jade and troops making them a regular enough sight in the lands between Juche and the Imperial City even while the Order maintains that relations between them and mortals are improper to the station of the latter. I get a sense here that the loyalty of those who dwell under the Blessed Isle to the Realm implies a degree of political distinction from others, but I\u2019m not sure if that\u2019s any different from what prior Editions did (although that may have been owed largely to saying that the majority lived around there). Like the Fair Folk, I\u2019ll be keeping my eye out for them.<br /> <br /> Okay, what are they saying about the prefecture itself\u2026 hmm, is the intention that it\u2019s distinguished from the outer cities by lacking larger settlements, balanced with a greater concentration of secondary and tertiary industries supported by the wealth of House Nellens? I like how their approach to subsidiary businesses finds a use for all comers, that even those who aren\u2019t financially sound enough to make good debtors can be beneficial to keep around to provide favours; it seems like it might be a less cutthroat environment in which businesses and entrepreneurs can have some breathing room to diversify and take risks. Certainly, given how those kinds of enterprises are the bread and butter of Nellens, it makes sense that such things would be pronounced in the heart of their power; in retrospect, it seems a bit of an oversight to have made that House so prominent here but not really have the locale feature the signature source of their wealth very much.<br /> <br /> Similarly, this close to the Imperial Mountain has generally been portrayed as somewhere that religious sentiments run close to the surface, so it also makes sense to depict it in that light. More than that, it just gives a lot of attractive local colour for it to be home to some especially old shrines and storied communities; the palace of the prefect done up in the style of a temple sounds especially gorgeous, I\u2019m really picturing it as built right up against the side of a mountain in a somewhat elevated position. I am also fond of the idea that temples and religious services form the core of mercantile activities, while the description of the regard held for local hermits smacks of it being an area that manages to possess some mildly heterodox denominations of the faith.<br /> <br /> Little reminder that this is where the Obsidian Mirror resides to accept young outcastes to be given preliminary training in their service to the Realm, and then continuing to develop how Nellens fosters relations among the mortals with a description of them roaming to personally deal with dangerous animals and criminals. I find a lot interesting in that; What Fire Has Wrought worked to make their traditional mercantile activities a bit more dynamic by tying it to the image of the travelled adventurer, and there are shades of that here, but it also sets a bit of a different tone to how they related to the commoners. Second Edition particularly laid down some ideas about Nellens presenting themselves as all friendly to and in the same boat as the common person who might find themselves \u201cscrewed over\u201d by the other Great Houses, but this suggests that the House has an interest in cultivating a measure of awe amongst the populace; I suspect that they still come across as more approachable and reasonable than, say, the likes of Ledaal, but I also read it as wanting to be admired in a warmer, closer manner. It\u2019s a strategy that I find very much reminiscent of what the magistrates do for the Empress, which makes me think about how magistrates are likely to have been among the founders of the House. That and the stated point about how it keeps them on guard and shortens response times in the event of an attack from neighbours; it\u2019s interesting how, between this and What Fire Has Wrought depicting the prefecture as being heavily fortified, this place that is so close to the literal centre of the Blessed Isle can feel so distinctly at risk, the implication that they\u2019ve never quite felt secure under the peace of the Empress. In any case, it certainly helps that the depiction of the Imperial Mountain as a source of monsters and the higher prominence for the Isle\u2019s banditry (who would surely find a lot of choice hiding places around these parts) would give such heroes a lot to do. And I enjoy the use of Spider\u2019s Crossing as the setting of a mysterious murder of one such wanderer that sets his companions and perhaps the House as a whole on edge; sure, one doesn\u2019t have to use the town having been formerly portrayed as a base of Iselsi to mean that the death the Nellens suspect to have been foul play orchestrated by a rival (probably Sesus) was in fact a disruptive instance of the Vendetta, but it can be where goes the mind of one that recognises the name and the significance it used to carry. I\u2019m not saying it was written with the intention of Iselsi machinations being a secret implication, but it\u2019s at least a blank space on which the notion can be easily projected.<br /> <br /> Ah yes, and I see that Juche itself has changed rather dramatically to reflect the altered character of its House and altered focus of its prefecture; no longer the site for processing and shipping stone reaped from the central mountains, inhabited primarily by masons and slaves and with a character of being a place where all Houses wanted some presence to stake a claim to the heart of the world. Its origins were never really focused on before, but it makes sense that it would date back so far as the First Age, and I can see the value of giving the Realm such an enduring city. Where Nellens before seemed a bit like they wound up in the city by default while they tried to find quarters in the Imperial Palace, being a more proactive home base means it makes sense that they instead direct such energies into beatifying the place for the sake of depicting their own tenuous pride and improve their standing among at least the commoners and the pious. No architectural style is described, but the gilded roofs, gardens and fountains and streets clear of rubbish and potholes gives me an image of El Dorado as depicted in the Dreamworks film (being a First Age city might help with that).<br /> <br /> But the big thing is building upon the religious significance. I very much enjoy real life instances of a religion transferring its centre for reasons of political expediency or simply just having found a far removed site to construct a more suitable home base, and the fictional narratives that reflect such things, and the history that has always been ascribed to the Immaculate Philosophy is a natural fit for such, so it\u2019s quite welcome here. It makes the Order feel a lot more alive to provide it with a central origin point, and while it\u2019s never exactly lacked for holy sites it is always something else to have the spots granted significance by being where things are perceived to have begun, the physical embodiments of the key events of scripture, and I find it more compelling to focus on how such spots predate the Realm itself. Especially for the implication from stating that some date to the Realm Before that at least a few of these were originally built towards different purposes, and find themselves being appropriated in service to the Dragon Blooded national cult; whatever its origin, I like the spot held to have been where Mela first marshalled resistance to the Anathema particularly (including how the red granite surface suggests some connotations with Mars to me). Although the idea of dedications to and relics from proponents of the faith at a time when it was not yet the familiar powerhouse are no slouch either. Oh, and how one of the most prominent temples houses sariras attributed to Hesiesh himself, that\u2019s neat. Centres of pilgrimage collect a lot of fees and donations as a matter of course, but it does feel like a bit of a synthesis of the city\u2019s inherent character with its current custodians in this instance. Heh, I can readily imagine that it was quite a shock for Nellens to have not only been formed by the Empress\u2019 edict, but to be granted this of all residences; kind of suggests to me both a thumbing of the nose at the rest of the Scarlet Dynasty and a perception that such a provocative House benefitted from being given a home where their peers might be reluctant to start any trouble. Oh and hey, House Nellens is only a hundred and seventy eight years old, there can be plenty of Exalted around who personally remember that controversy.<br /> <br /> What Fire Has Wrought didn\u2019t talk too much about the qualities of the mortal Nellens, but what it did say of them and the House as a whole is a good setup for Juche being home to a secondary school to train in legendary social graces; where else are they going to develop the skills to so effectively navigate the venomous society of the Scarlet Dynasty with apparent good humour and so well equipped to filter out friends, or at least partners. Oh and even that has such nuances; that they train their mortal scions to find their way into the galas and salons through attractive artistic skills, that\u2019s a very clever way to get into the good graces of their scornful peers. Yesterday I watched The Handmaiden for the first time ever, and it instilled in me an idea of some patrician from the sticks who finds herself in the employ of Exalted high society on the strength of her reputation for being able to give singularly beautiful readings of the Immaculate Texts, but Nellens has effectively usurped that position. It probably fits them a bit better anyway, since they\u2019d logically be sending in somebody with a bit more calculation. Hehehehe, the secret industry of House Nellens, the social climber. It\u2019s a field in which virtually all of the rest of the Dynasty is poorly equipped to compete.<br /> <br /> Damn, I really want to watch Downton Abbey now; I mean, my Mam\u2019s box set is literally right across from me\u2026 and Vanity Fair is on Netflix\u2026 <br /> <br /> What do you know, almost two thousand words on just Juche Prefecture, never expected that; that\u2019s about twice as much as I\u2019ve had to say about any prior prefecture besides the Scarlet one, and it doesn\u2019t even have the advantages of Imperial City and Palace. Rivan Quay might still be my favourite of the House home cities, but I think Juche might be standing out as my favourite updated version of something from prior Editions; What Fire Has Wrought gave a lot to enhance the ambition of House Nellens, but this is really strengthening their position by giving them a lot of key resources and processes to work with, and I think it can really facilitate some interesting and distinct stories for them. What I find particularly interesting is how the other cases mostly amount to the Houses making their home cities (even in cases where they didn\u2019t necessarily found them), whereas explicitly making Juche into a city much older than Nellens (especially when it\u2019s made several centuries younger than in previous Editions) really makes it feel like the House was made by its city, to the benefit of both of them; Juche feels more powerful for being able to exert such an influence (that and just sounding very nice and getting everything that comes from being an older Immaculate site), while Nellens feels capable of ascending from its nominally weak position by taking the wealth and significance and information provided by their home and leveraging that into numerous advantages that can come at the other Houses sideways. It\u2019s a fascinating symbiosis, unlike anything the other Houses do have, could have, with the possible exception of V\u2019neef when I get around to that. I think that one looks at the information on this prefecture, and stories for House Nellens practically write themselves. It\u2019s actually making me rethink their placement in my personal ranking of the Third Edition House write-ups.<br /> <br /> Aaaannd\u2026 actually I\u2019ll just leave it at that. I\u2019ll start the next one with the two sidebars before looking at Arjuf Dominion. It just feels proper to let Juche Prefecture have this space to itself.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-02T11:42:36-07:00","id":1339030,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1336696-finishing-reading-the-realm?p=1338204#post1338204\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I also find the House to strike an interesting balance in its relationship with the local commoners; that they\u2019re not exactly loved or have any love themselves, but at least value enough stability to make sure that things run without overt cause for objection. I like seeing the House\u2019s Aspect in this policy, and it makes for a very strong contrast with House Mnemon, how their Earth Aspect in Djelis comes from promoting spirituality and investing the common people in the value of that traditionalism, where Ragara just wants things to be efficient enough that they remain static. I also like how, for all of this, there remains an underlying tension owing to them being unable to quite shake the legacy of their matriarch being basically the worst human; I\u2019m kind of imagining a scenario in which Ragara actually does finally die, not of anything dramatic or actionable, his life just finally gives out, and an attempt to hold a public funeral for him ends up provoking a really massive riot. Not because of something specific, just because there can\u2019t help but being this massive release of backed up energy when there\u2019s a really old history of bad blood that was quietly backed up over such a long period of lacking the catharsis of him ever having actually died.</div> </div> </div> </div>Like a less civil and more volatile version of <a href=\"https://www.bbc.com/news/entertainment-arts-22145306\" target=\"_blank\">this</a>?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1336696-finishing-reading-the-realm?p=1338204#post1338204\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The What Fire Has Wrought write-up also had this really nice metaphor about the Imperial Mountain rising proudly but having dark secrets beneath its roots that made for a nice image of the House itself, and I like seeing that also reflected in their homeland in the form of a lot of overgrown ghost towns and exhausted, closed off mines that not only accumulate a lot of vagabonds and people on the margins (I don\u2019t think mines have ever been something historically poached as such, but poachers is the image I\u2019m getting of how the House would treat prospectors who try to eke out some scraps that aren\u2019t worth the trouble to Dynasts but could make a peasant comfortably well-off), but how they try to dig out any lost magics that might be lurking beneath the surface. Recovery in the aftermath of an earthquake is an unexpected inciting incident for them, and I like how their concern isn\u2019t just about anything powerful that might have been opened up, but needing to figure out how to regain access to productive mines that have been buried.</div> </div> </div> </div><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serra_Pelada\" target=\"_blank\">Serra Pelada</a> has a number of stories that might border, if not outright enter, into actual mine poaching. I know it's current status involves a decades-long dispute with government authorities over concessions, how depleted or not the place actually is among other things.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1336696-finishing-reading-the-realm?p=1338204#post1338204\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Riven Quay would be all well and good as an industrial centre, but I really love how it marries that to the point of being a brownwater port for the sake of gathering the transported raw minerals to give the city a distinct character with spiritual cleansing imagery as a necessity of life amid all of the smog and smells, while also being another avenue for the expression of wealth in terms of things like what you spend in shielding your home from the general unpleasantness and going about smelling of roses. It\u2019s like\u2026 Shinto cleansing practices blended with Roman baths blended with London\u2019s concerns with the purity of the Thames, it\u2019s very clever. It also feels like it would have a plausible basis for a higher focus on heresy than the other overcrowded cities when its issues create greater pervasive health detriments, even before getting to the point of how Ragara lets it slide so that Immaculates are more occupied with suppressing god worshippers than realise that the ruling House flirts with blasphemy. Ragara might not have been my favourite new take on a House (and it was still pretty good), but I think this is my favourite take thus far on how a House relates to its home prefecture and city.</div> </div> </div> </div>Quite interesting set-up and goes to show how much of a dirty manipulator and all-around utterly awful being Ragara can very much be even in retirement age, specially in it, might be said.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-07T18:50:17-07:00","id":1340129,"title":"","content":"Thankfully short sidebars to begin with now. I could have sworn that the Dragonswrath Desert was one of the things featured in the Kickstarter preview last year but it doesn\u2019t seem to match\u2026 perhaps it was just redrafted at a later point. Anyway, my thoughts are still liking the Blessed Isle to have a sizable desert to provide some stark variety to its landscape and a noteworthy refuge for the marginalized, with the early makings of its own nomadic refugee community synergizing particularly well with writing for the Lunars that has come up in the interim. The description of mysterious ancient ruins characterized by local legend and apparently a source of concern for Immaculates would serve well just as a bit of colour and backdrop to any stories that might intersect with the region, but with hooks given about Exalted archaeologists I\u2019d also call them a draw for those stories that can have a different tone and look from what is typical of the Realm without having to venture from its confines, which I\u2019d say would be very helpful for Dynast stories that are heavily rooted there. Heck for that matter, all of those details also help it to be a place where Solars could go about doing something useful without needing to constantly worry about Wyld Hunts.<br /> <br /> I\u2019m reading Jinitama\u2019s less as a plot hook and more as an anecdotal snapshot of the current state of the Blessed Isle, as a place where rising tensions and insecurity could cause a community to start turning in on itself and then uniting to cover up a secret that is both shameful and a material threat to their well being. I don\u2019t mean that critically, I think that such a thing can be useful to letting the Realm feel like it has an inner life and stakes taking place at a scale below the notice of a lot of the Exalted, and it could certainly still be the seed of a story in which a magistrate or Dynast or any other outsider shows up and starts to notice that the villagers are acting very cagey. I also think it\u2019s a novel instance of how the Anathema label might be misappropriated by people seeking rationalization for their own moral shortcomings; one might elect to use that in a story about the Realm in which such a hysteria becomes a more widespread phenomenon.<br /> <br /> On the other side, I like the way that Kizuna Prefecture uses a single disaster (that may or may not be natural and arbitrary) as the inciting incident for any number of plot hooks ranging from the banal practicalities of infrastructure maintenance to being incorporated into the rhetoric of contrary political positions to the more strange and esoteric. I like how much of that last one in particular is phrased in terms of local rumours rather than definitive facts, since it can speak to the general mindset of Realm citizens as well as being a basis for further political incidents, and the most overtly existing supernatural phenomenon has the benefit of appearing to be a fun little Black Company reference (a thing I might not otherwise think to locate on the Blessed Isle despite how much amusing mayhem such could cause in these trying times). I\u2019m not quite sure why House Peleps is on the hook for a prefecture they place no value in, but I elect to see that as raising questions worth devising answers to rather than complaining about; my preliminary idea is that it\u2019s the result of some obscure political manoeuvring from the past that they found themselves stuck with and unable to get shot of. Whatever the reason, it does bring up an interesting image of Great Houses being invested in prefectures that they\u2019re liable to ignore or neglect during a period where such a thing could really come back to haunt them even if it doesn\u2019t involve literal vengeful ghosts.<br /> <br /> And Trove\u2026 I don\u2019t really have anything to say about Trove. There\u2019s nothing wrong with it, it\u2019s just not sparking my interest much. Perfectly serviceable plot hook about a local abuse of power to profit off of human misery. Errr\u2026 huh, I was going to make a comment about \u201cBucolic Hymn\u201d being an amusing and perhaps somewhat embarrassing name for a prefecture, but now I\u2019ve gone and learned a new word referring to pleasant aspects of countryside and country life, so it\u2019s at least fitting.<br /> <br /> Now on to Arjuf Dominion, famed as the home of those awful bastards menacing those lovely Nellens folks. To me\u2026 at the moment\u2026 perhaps I\u2019ve been rendered a bit prejudiced\u2026 Anyway, gets right off the bat in displaying how pervasive the revised attitudes of House Ledaal are with their habit of managing regular patrols of the populace to ensure their compliance with law and upright conduct. Hmm, also seems to be running along similar lines to Numinous Rolling Waves, albeit where the stakes of maintaining the geomancy aren\u2019t keeping the Defence Grid functional but ensuring that their heartlands maintains that appropriately manicured look to it. I\u2019m kind of getting a sense of the Dominion\u2019s geomancy being an intersection between Ledaal\u2019s familiar occult expertise and new authoritarianism, with just a touch of ego to it; the kind of thing that I might have once attributed to House Mnemon. I\u2019m not entirely sure what it means by the practice rarely being employed elsewhere, though; is it saying that places besides villages within the Dominion are rarely laid out in tenuous geomancy, or that places outside the Dominion rarely employ such standards?<br /> <br /> It\u2019s nice to see that Arjuf is still a major financial centre for the Realm, with what feels to me like an even stronger emphasis on institutions that I often related to the Edo rice markets than it had before. I like it both for laying out such details clearly to be picked up on by general readers, emphasizing that agricultural disaster can still be an opportunity for the unscrupulous to benefit, and a few processes by which such disasters might occur (or be deliberately caused by somebody with an agenda, whether targeted at the Realm, the House, or the principalities of the South). We were already told about how Ledaal were harsher and more distant overlords than is typical of even Dynasts, but that\u2019s now complemented with the experience that forms for the people who have been most thoroughly and consistently subjected to it; I think that the peasants get an effective mix of perspectives between people who\u2019ll allow that, for all their lacking in sentiment the Ledaal\u2019s at least seem to take the ideals of noblesse oblige seriously enough to make life comfortable for those commoners who toe the line without fear of Exalted abuse (while implying that enough people have felt the brunt of uncompromising Ledaal \u201cjustice\u201d that uprisings are not uncommon), and I find it interesting for Arjuf to seemingly be a land where the lesser aristocracy are actually the more oppressed class (if only in the form of having privileges that might be taken for granted elsewhere withheld from them; I kind of get the feeling that if the House doesn\u2019t grossly abuse their power, they at least leverage the advantage of controlling such a lucrative and attractive area).<br /> <br /> I\u2019m fully on board with Ragara being an all-around bastard, but like First Edition Mnemon before him I like it when such a character has at least one humanising feature, and I can think of little better than the idea that his father\u2019s tomb is something that he paid for and regularly visited. I also feel sentimental about the notion of it as something that he and Mnemon collaborated upon (however distantly that might have been), because the idea of them having something in common and a shared achievement gives their sibling relationship a bit more feeling to me. Even the point that they wouldn\u2019t visit it at the same time makes it stand out to me as being at least one instance in all the world where their connection is expressed less as fierce political rivals and more as deeply embittered family, and that is endearing to me. It\u2019s also cute how such a focal point is somewhere that members of the two Houses can actually be expected to get along, even if it\u2019s only known of (perhaps even functional with) the non-Exalted scions, although perhaps that\u2019s simply an emergent phenomenon of the mortals being the ones you\u2019d actually expect to retire in such numbers for a community to form. Despite my previous allusion, it is a bit easy for me to forget with the details of House Ledaal\u2019s character provided by this book that What Fire Has Wrought presented them in a dangerously isolated position, so I\u2019m glad to have this point that responsibility for a monument precious to two Houses traditionally at each other\u2019s throats is a basis for them remaining on the good side of some of their peers (with contemporary failure to do so compounding their problems of the time).<br /> <br /> Hmm, interesting to provide the austere Ledaal with their own equivalent to the Delphic Oracle, rendered even more uncanny and inscrutable by not being burdened by any apparent physical form. The Empress having consulted it simultaneously covers my enjoying images of her as having favoured advisors or court augurs and actively pursuing the expansion of her personal arsenal of ancient magic, and I like the juxtaposition of the mundane and mystical in the idea that House Ragara presents a tempting offer of debt cancellation for the beleaguered House in exchange for exclusive access, but I\u2019d still find Ledaal to have the most interesting relationship with this thing. I might expect them to view it as inauspicious or heretical, but I\u2019m compelled by how it instead represents an instance of the shadow of their founding in the ruin of treacherous Jurul looming over them; that they fear the possibility that their members might lack the resolve to not fall for similar temptations. Either that or an instance where fear of following such a path makes them irrationally afraid of something deeply connected to Ledaal\u2019s mother (even if it being able to influence petitioners is apparently a matter of fact), both approaches are good.<br /> <br /> I\u2019m liking this write-up, but I don\u2019t quite feel as though it\u2019s conveying the purpose or significance of this being a Dominion\u2026 hmm, even saying that though, I kind of feel compelled to find my own reason in the form of something like \u201cArjuf being their major gateway to the south and southwest necessitates providing it with a lot of agricultural and geomantic resources (in a quadrant of the Isle that is otherwise more arid) to ensure that it has an abundance of produce to allow advantage in some of the most lucrative trade in the world\u201d. Yyyeeaaahh that\u2019ll work.<br /> <br /> Speaking of that extremely important port: seeing as it\u2019s the de facto second city of the Realm, it\u2019s fitting for it to start off as being another place decorated with monuments acquired directly from the imperialism. Looking back, it\u2019s funny that Arjuf has been talked about as a big deal but not really described in any great detail in its own right. I think it\u2019s a strong start to describe that initial wave of refugees from the Seven Tigers as forming both the political foundation of its place in the structure of the Realm and the ethnic foundation of its majority inhabitants. It was all well and good to have an overt point about the Blessed Isle having greater ethnic diversity, but I think the point is made especially well by having an example of that being evident in one of the most prominent locales.<br /> <br /> Heh, the idea of the major port city being advantaged by possessing a special form of autonomy and semi-democracy (maybe elective oligarchy is a more accurate description) from the larger royal authority touched a soft spot from a sense of familiarity that I couldn\u2019t initially identify, but I\u2019ve just realised that it\u2019s because that\u2019s basically an idea I had for a coastal city from a time I tried imagining a more elaborate version of Hyrule from the Legend of Zelda series some\u2026 nine years ago, Jesus. Anyway, I like free cities in larger empires being around to shake up the official social dynamics, and combined with being connected to the wealth and diversity inherent to being a major trade city it makes Arjuf more interesting as a nexus of power and intrigue standing out a bit from the Realm surrounding it. It\u2019s a potential that\u2019s certainly capitalised upon with the backstory of governors encouraging a variety of intellectuals flocking to the city in a manner that made it a sanctuary for heterodox thinkers in the otherwise generally conforming Realm. Makes for an especially good tension with the new take on Ledaal at the heart of their domain, and I can imagine that stories focused on the new scenario of a dedicated Ledaal governor cracking down on all of that free-thinking might resonate with a lot of players. I\u2019m also amused by the idea that even then, the House enforcers are unusually restrained in dealing with the likes of rioting students because that\u2019s still an environment where they might find themselves on the wrong side of a scion of the Dynasty (presumably, Ledaal won\u2019t go to bat for any officer who damages a Dynast even if they were doing so on the House\u2019s orders).<br /> <br /> Makes sense that they would revise the old presentation of the city having remained the headquarters of the Merchant Fleet even after it was transferred to the control of V\u2019neef, and I think it\u2019s clever to use that as the basis for a new feature of a large series of docks that have only recently had their long-established function lost, adding even more to Arjuf\u2019s tumults by creating an unruly new dock district. I mean, probably unruly even by the usual standards of such places; I imagine that Ledaal hasn\u2019t exactly had the luxury or infrastructure to have leased out the newly opened space in a more discriminating manner or to larger scale enterprises. It makes me think that the place might have become even more porous to entry by typically unwelcome entrants to the Blessed Isle; I enjoy the irony of thinking that Ledaal of all Houses might have created a significant gap through which Anathema might slip.<br /> <br /> Let\u2019s see, what else\u2026 gives a more firm picture of what kinds of distinctly southern products can be found in the markets, which should hook nicely to any information we\u2019re eventually given about outgoing goods from the likes of Chiaroscuro. Same goes with some of the specialized varieties of mystics and intellectuals that come from those cities and nations. I\u2019m kind of getting an image somewhere between the parade of hawkers typical of fictional scenes of a person walking through a marketplace and that array of prophets shows in that one scene of Monty Python\u2019s Life of Brian in the description of how the philosophers, alchemists and astrologers offer services to passersby while waiting to be recruited by a Ledaal. It\u2019s curious, it\u2019s a fairly throwaway line but I find myself particularly drawn to it\u2026 I think it\u2019s because I\u2019m seeing it from the point of view of one of those people trying to make a particularly good show alongside everybody else when a Ledaal actually does come looking around, and seething with jealousy when the Exalt wordlessly selects somebody else who practically weeps with joy at being able to leave the streets and take up service in one of the big houses. Maybe I\u2019m just processing the idea through the part of me that is fond of Regency/Victorian drama, maybe it\u2019s just reminiscent of all of those accounts I read in history classes about poor Irish children looking for work at the hiring fairs. I guess I\u2019m mostly just taking some amusement in placing such an apparently prestigious and high class profession in that fraught and degrading position.<br /> <br /> Hahaha, actually, do you know what it reminds me of? C-3PO being part of a line up of tech farm labourers and needing to talk up how his communication skills can make him useful as a farm hand. I\u2019ve never really thought about that scene in those terms before, the juxtaposition between him being so prim and proper while engaging in a scene straight out of Of Mice and Men (I presume).<br /> <br /> Moving on, given how the developers have pointed out elsewhere that even the home prefectures of Great Houses are the residence of a mix of families, it seems to me that the point of most within the city being members of Ledaal is meant to stand out as disproportionate even by the standards of such a headquarters. I\u2019m curious about the implications this have for the city only recently having a governor that so overtly serves their interests when the franchise is reserved for Dragon Blooded; I\u2019m alternating between the supervision of the Empress meaning that the Ledaal majority were never bold enough to abuse their electoral powers to do anything to undermine the imperial grant of autonomy, and that most Dynasts being Ledaal doesn\u2019t preclude a lot of resident outcastes who can tip the scales. Oh wait\u2026 no, silly me, the most obvious reconciliation is to remember that the city of Arjuf (even as a part of the Realm) predates the very existence of House Ledaal by more than four hundred years (I forgot to mention before, but I like the city\u2019s significance being emphasized by it being another that pointedlyly predates the Realm). Even if Arjuf Dominion was the residence of House Jurul, and even if I was to assume that the predecessor House possessed a similar conservative character, that House can\u2019t possibly have existed before Nellens became imperial consort, and that only happened in year 365. So yeah, Arjuf just gets those governors who cultivate that character during the first half of the Realm\u2019s existence, and by the time that Ledaal is granted the surrounding lands and becomes a disproportionate number of the voting residents, the culture is very firmly established; if they weren\u2019t electing governors who might actively attempt to overturn it (still probably a move that would invite reprisal from the Empress), I can imagine that the centuries in which the House has dominated have seen many governors who didn\u2019t exactly do it any favours. In any case, it\u2019s an interesting idea that, even while the House disapproves of the general liberalism of the city, it\u2019s still too much of a major expression of wealth and culture for them to pass up taking part in, not to mention the benefit of being able to readily range out into the Threshold for the Shadow Crusade.<br /> <br /> Nice to see that it still has that point about how most of the local captains and guides are on retainer to keep Ledaal informed of the voyages of fellow Dynasts. And I\u2019m glad that description for the House of Bells is kept to such a minimum, with just the point that citizens are the pool the academy draws on to provide the troops for the mock battles; I suppose that has to bring an especially unique form of excitement to the lives of any Realm citizens, perhaps even a form of prestige if they talk themselves up as aiding in the training of the next generation of great holy warriors for the empire\u2019s defence and expansion. If fatalities in the exercises are typically a result of students Exalting, I have to imagine that such deaths are rather uncommon overall.<br /> <br /> This artwork\u2026 I feel as though it\u2019s depicting something a bit further on, so I\u2019ll come back to it.<br /> <br /> Tuchara is another one that has received a rather significant overhaul from an artist\u2019s colony into a somewhat unconventional industrial town. I think I\u2019ve brought up before how I\u2019m interested in giving prominence to the use of amphorae as storage for mass transportation of fluid goods, so I like seeing that being referenced in relation to the Realm\u2019s premier shipping outlet. I get the impression from it being described as having began as a pottery-making town that it started off as a place making straightforward decorative objects that became adapted to serving those shipping needs, so it touches on a few points of interest such as a location depicted as having changed over time in response to economic incentives and giving prominence to something pivotal that might be often overlooked. I feel as though the nature of its core industry might also lend itself to the placid nature of the city that various supplementary businesses can play off of, although I\u2019m saying that from a position of not knowing much about ceramics production (such as whether or not they\u2019d be using kilns, and what kilns at that scale might do for local air and water quality); something to look into. In any case, it sounds like another city that has invested itself in being attractive and distinctive to affluent visitors, a trend in the description of Realm cities that I\u2019ve been enjoying; it makes them easier to imagine and likely to tell apart in conversation, I\u2019d say it makes them more compelling to Storytellers to use as backdrops for any given session, and they add to the characterization of the Realm\u2019s citizens and local government that they might be encouraged to engage in civic projects that make them stand out among fellows. In that spirit, Tuchara seems well suited as the home for a fallen Great House that might hope to rise again; they\u2019ve got an efficient but unobtrusive basis for wealth and drawing visitors to feel out as benefactors.<br /> <br /> There\u2019s a word, there\u2019s an adjective I know that describes the kinds of events that the Blue Star Festival is, but I just can\u2019t bring it to mind\u2026 bacchanalian, I think that was it. So yeah, I wondered if the Realm might have any such event, that instance of pious deprivation followed by intense bacchanalian revelry akin to medieval Christmas. Considering that this isn\u2019t an older city, I wonder if the practice is something that predates the Realm and took root here in defiance of Immaculate tutting, or if it\u2019s part and parcel with the development of the city\u2019s service industry. It might be a very confined instance of such in the wider scope of the Blessed Isle, but I can imagine that even the distant promise of being able to get the means and permission to make the pilgrimage there can be a motivation and a relief to many peasants. I like the idiom associated with children conceived at the time, plus how the sheer density of attendants makes it impractical for monk chaperones to prevent any untoward conduct. There\u2019s something distinctly compelling about the idea that the number and poor attention spans who gather at the time provides a huge infusion of cash for the local criminal element, and especially the process they have for laundering that money back to themselves in manageable amounts that don\u2019t draw too much attention (I wonder if any group in real life has functioned in quite that manner). It\u2019s fun how that gives the city a capacity to possess nouveau riche that wouldn\u2019t be found elsewhere in the Realm, complete with pockmarking the landscape with grand mansions falling to ruin from owners that didn\u2019t know how to manage that wealth properly.<br /> <br /> With that context, I\u2019ll say of the illustration that I like how it depicts people engaged in the festivities across a mix of age ranges to make it feel more widespread, the costumes look nicely rich and ornate, and those fireworks seem as though they\u2019d lend an especially grand quality to the proceedings. Mind, the lanterns hanging over them really ought to be blue; quite apart from that being the established colour for such things in much of Creation, it is after all in the name.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-10T20:52:41-07:00","id":1340715,"title":"","content":"Ahhh right, the Tarpan Wastes was that desert in the Kickstarter preview. I believe that what I said back then was that I think it does a lot to give the Blessed Isle this desert that has only appeared and gradually expanded during the Realm Era, such as giving a very solid focus of the world having changed over time (although admittedly that doesn\u2019t quite have the impact in a book that is full of such things) and adding a hostile environment in a key spot that the resident societies are still not quite adapted to (although still not strictly suffering from, what with taking advantage of the sand for glass production and exposed salt flats to sell that commodity).<br /> <br /> Hmm, the line describing the reputation of the inhabitants stands out to me in an odd way now. I like the point of how the hardship brings out a positive attitude in people that one might not expect, to the point that it subverts the usual Realm standards of decorum (possibly helped by an implicit lack of Exalted residents or attention), but the prose strikes me as flowery in a manner that is not only uncharacteristic of the rest of the chapter, but doesn\u2019t exactly fit the rest of this entry. I doubt it would have stood out to me the first time I read it, but with the tone established by all of the writing before it takes me out of things a bit.<br /> <br /> Huh, looking at the paragraph about the Salt Road, I\u2019m actually now feeling as though my original assessment of the region was mistaken when I lacked the wider context of the Blessed Isle. The place has been clearly diminished, but here we\u2019re given a point that it still holds a significant economic and strategic value in providing a bypass for Arjuf Dominion, to the point that House Mnemon maintains the road networks and sorcerers are employed to ensure that travellers will have fresh water, even though that infrastructure needs to be frequently refreshed. Between that any the description of the manufacturing output, I\u2019m getting less of an impression of a region fallen on hard times due to ecological disaster and more of a place that just shifted its niche to one that still benefits its inhabitants, even if it\u2019s undeniably a harsher place to live. I still get an impression that it\u2019s an assignment for the power players to often avoid, but it otherwise feels like a more high-functioning region.<br /> <br /> But it still has the transition having caused a major disruption to the supernatural landscape. I think that, even in-character, the question of what becomes of gods in response to major environmental changes is a bit of an elephant in the room. Yu-Shan at least has the convenience of presenting sections of the divine city as degenerating into slums, but Creation lacks an obvious place for them to end up, and I like how that is made a central focus here; that a lot of gods find themselves without anything to care for and are stuck between trying to dodge around Immaculates (who in this case might be seen to have a valid point in preventing disruption and competition among established hierarchies of gods and relationships with their communities) or stuck playing out defunct roles. It\u2019s a situation that makes the appearance of a politically significant literal dragon pop; it\u2019s one thing for such a dramatic and iconic monster to swoop down and demand tribute from the locals in the style of Tanith Lee\u2019s Flat Earth, it\u2019s another for them to serve as a focal point for disenfranchised spirits. I find it to be an effective use of how elementals grow in power over time as compared to gods, that an elemental dragon can be something new and powerful to shake things up, while still part of the spiritual ecology (I do think the last line of that paragraph is a bit awkwardly written though, it sounds like setting up different sides of a point that are actually just the same point). You\u2019d expect it to be a focus of ghosts, but I think it both compounds the pathos of the gradual environmental breakdown by having a lot of the ghosts wanting to move on but not knowing how to, and complements the capacity for being an area where traditions break down by them openly offering services to the living and seeking alliance with the dragon. And I\u2019ve got a soft spot for deserts being homes to dangerous ghosts and spirits that has some kind of fortress or monastery resting at its border dedicated to keeping them in check, nicely connected to the current political concerns by having a lot of those stationed there going off for a more attractive service. The approach to manses leaves some interesting ruins to dot the landscape as well as what I find one of the stronger instances of House Mnemon talents being put to use (being able to save a manse under threat feels like a more demanding feat even than building an entirely new one), and the Well of Holy Fire intrigues me with its collection of ambiguous features in a state of decay and the implication that some sinister entity is operating around it. The Well actually feels like something that the Blessed Isle could use a bit more of, things that feel as though they\u2019d be ancient beyond human history and emphasize that it\u2019s more than just the big continent in the middle of the world, it was the original homeland of the gods and possible focus of other powers.<br /> <br /> As for Hualli and Grip, I think it\u2019s always nice to include a few odder Exalted and especially among the Dragon Blooded given how they\u2019re such a larger group that can cover a lot of outliers, and they\u2019re well matched to such a tumultuous corner of the map as this. I like how Hualli is more than just a beastperson with an especially vivid appearance (I would still picture him resembling Kass the Rito from Breath of the Wild) but that he\u2019s certainly got some stories to tell from being possibly more well-travelled than a lot of Celestial Exalted, which might also really emphasize commitment to his mission on the Blessed Isle that could put a lot of local peasants at ease with him (I\u2019m suddenly thinking that might have been part of why he was received as a celebrity rather than a figure of scorn, although I guess even in a society that would generally reject beastfolk some of them are just way too pretty or exotic looking). Grip unfortunately can\u2019t help but feel a little bit dull beside that, but I think she plays off well with my feeling from the Legion of Silence write-up that it gave the members more agency and humanity lying behind their inscrutable fa\u00e7ade, to have her feel empowered by Exaltation to reject the life she was brought up for and apply her gifts to one more focused on peace, contemplation and beauty.<br /> <br /> I\u2019ve been seeing glimpses of this \u201cA Memory of Empire\u201d sidebar the last few times I\u2019ve looked around here and it intrigues me, so I\u2019ll go straight for that.<br /> <br /> Hmm\u2026 honestly, my feelings are a bit mixed. On the one hand, I like the idea of an ethnic group asserting that the Blessed Isle was once home to an empire functioning separate from the Shogunate until their terrible downfall as a result of Exaltation rate having dramatically dropped off, who survive as pastoral nomads. It\u2019s a nice quirk in the setting\u2019s history to suggest that the major government of the world hasn\u2019t always reigned from the Blessed Isle, I like an inclusion of a state\u2019s failure coming from the potential for Terrestrial bloodlines to fail (possibly the kind of thing that haunts the Great Houses), and that kind of enduring national resentment of a group given a raw deal feels fitting as something that could be found even in the Realm\u2019s heartlands. However, the manner in which it phrases these things in terms of heresy and blasphemy takes me a bit out of it when I don\u2019t feel as though there\u2019s a clear sense of the nature of those things here. I\u2019m all for ambiguity and leaving gaps to be filled in, but this feels too much to me like an omission (when it\u2019s described as a basis for active persecution, I feel as though I need to know what the Immaculates are targeting), and placed alongside the more clearly stated elements it weakens the subject of the sidebar to me.<br /> <br /> Okay, Justiciar Prefecture. My, the actual place itself is short. I\u2019m feeling some more mixed feelings about this one; I get that some prefectures would be fairly placid and not distinguish themselves from their neighbours, and don\u2019t think that\u2019s inherently unworthy of focus, but it does create a bit of an uphill battle to make it interesting. Fair enough that it\u2019s described as being distinguished by a lower Immaculate presence that makes it happen to be a region with a higher than usual capacity for heresy, but I don\u2019t really get why. I can even accept the idea that there\u2019s no deeper underlying reason behind it, if it was just a situation of \u201clogistics happens to be playing out in a manner that leaves this place overlooked and an emergent property of that allows it to foster deviance\u201d, but I can\u2019t help but feel as though such a conceit would feel stronger if it was text.<br /> <br /> The city of Justiciar has a lot more going for it. If this is supposed to be a portion of the Isle that is a bit more uniform and uninteresting with lower development, I think it\u2019s a neat idea that one of the prefectures would specialize in providing administrative services for its neighbours. It touches on elements of specialization that I\u2019m liking in this rendition of the Realm, as well as appealing to familiar matters of rural areas sometimes having problems getting much government service. I also like how this shapes the urban culture towards being very crepuscular with the secondary industry being service for the bureaucrats on their off-hours, as well as how their demanding schedules forms the basis for a thriving dining scene. It uses the way that business would grow around a primary industry of paper pushing to make a distinct city in an otherwise unremarkable prefecture.<br /> <br /> Yuktata confuses me a bit, however. We\u2019re suddenly introduced to the idea that various forms of administrative malfeasance here are so common that they stir the wrath of a powerful goddess of justice to the point of her physically lashing out. The official response to both of these things is for the monks to throw major festivals to her on a weekly basis? Some of this feels at odds with how the institutions of the Realm are generally portrayed as dealing with problem officers and especially with the gods.<br /> <br /> Well, confused may be a bit of an extreme term; it\u2019s not hard for me to put together a functional explanation for all of this. I\u2019ve already presupposed that the western quadrant of the Blessed Isle is somewhat more impoverished, less infrastructurally developed and with less accommodating geography (I privately refer to it as the Connacht of the Blessed Isle). So with that combined with some other things said about the prefecture and the city, there\u2019s a basis for thinking that we\u2019re out in the boonies where they need to make their own rules a bit; hence a number of prefectures outsourcing their administration to a consolidated centre. That much concentration might be a natural breeding ground for a lot of graft and corruption, enough to provoke a local deity offended by those things, and be a bit difficult to effectively root out with the limited oversight they\u2019re given. And if the local Immaculates are simply underfunded, they might be forced to cope with such a thing in the best way that they can even if it might invite censure from their superiors. I can imagine such an explanation, and I think it covers most of the bases, but I wouldn\u2019t mind such a thing being more overt in the text, it would make the section read a bit cleaner to me. Although I\u2019m now wondering if I initially underestimated Yuktata, and her being referred to as the regional goddess of justice is meant to imply a spirit more on the level with somebody like Ahlat, tricky for even the Realm to restrain; in fairness, the optics might be bad when the spirit that you\u2019re opting to wrestle into submission is one who was acting so provocatively because so much injustice was rampant within your borders. For that matter, if her purview had that scope, I suppose she would be being pushed by more than just what\u2019s going on in this city.<br /> <br /> Anyway, moving on from that we\u2019ve got a prefect who would seem to further evince it being a bit of a wild west out here, both with feeling a need to have such a protective detail and with the fact that such private troops ruffle so many feathers with the local official police; I think those kinds of stories of jurisdictional friction crossed with outright physical violence (ala Tammany Hall New York) are often a lark. And if Iselsi felt distinctly absent from their home prefecture, I\u2019m very gratified to see them display a presence in a former holding so that they\u2019re in a prime position to strike unawares. I especially like a bit of a nod to previous depictions making them very prominent in the Order with one living under the cover of being a monk and using sand garden drawings to pass coded information and instructions to her network that can easily and unobtrusively be destroyed.<br /> <br /> For the artwork, it\u2019s a gorgeous depiction of that kind of rocky landscape for a start. I think the building has a mix of architectural influences that makes it look familiar but not quite like any one real building, and I like how the overall picture is very brightly coloured but the scheme on the building makes it stand out distinctly from the surroundings. There\u2019s also some very nice lighting effects, from glows reflecting off the surface of the streams and river and the look of a slight mist rising up and creating a distorting effect. The man on the raft gives a sense of scale for just how large the construction is, and the impression I get of the scene is of an idyllic, out of the way part of the Realm that nevertheless features a monumental reminder of the majesty of its rulers; it\u2019s not just here as an expression of piety, but for when people drift by on this river or turn up to do their laundry they\u2019ll witness yet another representation of the strength and glory of the Exalted. <br /> <br /> Gonna finish up for now with the next places of interest sidebar. With Brilliant Autumn Shades Prefecture, I\u2019m liking how often we\u2019re getting prefectures that are home to creepy ghost stories or similar dark secrets. It\u2019s nice to see a settled outcaste be the origin of such this time, as well as just a bit of representation for their impact in general. Murex Blaze has got a nice mix of establishing herself with the local gentry through generosity and favourable marriages (it\u2019s a fun turning on the head of a typical dynamic, where the nouveau riche might want to build credibility by marrying into the older families, where here it\u2019s an Exalt showing up and the old money are super juiced to welcome her children) with copious applications of evil magic. The idea of the big fish in the small backwater building up and maintaining their prominence with black sorcery is another fond motif to me, the whole blood magic and cannibalism angle invokes a lot of freaky folklore and some compelling stories based off such (for me that includes some nice Mage: the Awakening stuff), and while the narrative of the foreigner who comes in with exotic weirdness that they use to distort and bend the local natural order has some problematic connotations, it\u2019s still got a lot of merits and pedigree. I find something gratifying in how the thorough destruction of the family was spread out over several stages, and as elsewhere I\u2019m really feeling this approach of presenting hooks surrounding lingering ghosts and their chicanery in terms of the local rumour and hearsay (with just a dash of another resident sorcerer who has his own agendas). It\u2019s more sophisticated than just \u201cthis may or may not be true\u201d; there\u2019s definitely something going on, but recognition of what it is is distorted through the lens of observers who may be ignorant and a sinister legacy that people will naturally attribute it to. You combine that with descriptions of other phenomena on the Isle and this could be anything from \u201cthings are exactly as people describe them\u201d to \u201cit\u2019s still ghosts, but sharing stories has distorted the facts\u201d all the way over to \u201cthere\u2019s an unrelated root to it all, but people are going to reason it emerges from the dark local history\u201d. It\u2019s a very interesting way to put oddities and mysteries such as this in the Realm.<br /> <br /> You go from that to something much more grounded and direct in Damson Prefecture. I like seeing House Tepet apply their modus operandi for buffer groups in the Threshold to at least one of their problem holdings on the Blessed Isle in settling experienced soldiers in an area plagued with banditry. I also think it gives the much beleaguered House a much needed little victory to say that, even if they had to sell their stakes in the territory to House Ragara, they were sure to take their devoted former soldiers with them to both shore up their own forces for the future and thumb their noses at the new lease holders. It\u2019s fun to see Ragara wrong-footed, and I like the mixed reaction they have to the event in the form of being annoyed at the loss of people who were integral to keeping the peace but also being glad to see the back of ones so loyal to a rival and nervous around the ones that remained. It seems like it would be fun to play a Ragara having dealings with such freeholders while uncertain of their priorities.<br /> <br /> And Nine Envies Prefecture would appear to be one in which Cynis concentrates a lot of its agricultural operation, which I feel as though makes it a very unusual prefecture in terms of its economics and demographics while being unremarkable in its environment, a little island of dramatically distinct agrarian practices lying unobtrusively among the rushes (at least barring the occasional exodus or revolt of the disproportionately large slave populace). That\u2019s a solid plot hook with them dealing with the aftermath of a recent uprising that has one leader held captive and a lot of power with the free ones who might push things into a major insurgency. Gavenne of Grieve being locked up looks like a nice focus for stories of devotees plotting to release\u2026 them (I\u2019m just now noticing that none of the leaders are explicitly gendered, and as far as I know the names aren\u2019t clear indicators), or fallout of Cynis putting on some very public reprimand. While Lark seems to present the interesting dilemma of what to do with the Exalted slave in the process of defiance; official protocol probably holds that the priority should be placed on bringing them on for imperial service while the local Cynis would probably prefer quickly and quietly eliminating somebody who is kicking up such a fuss, and it seems ripe for representatives of the Arbiters to be present to insist that Lark be taken in alive (a tension that can be compounded if the reps are monks with their traditional antipathy for the ways of Cynis). It\u2019s a nice little thorn to stick in the side of their organisation.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-25T08:43:22-07:00","id":1343592,"title":"","content":"Eagerly awaiting more!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Developer for Exalted.<br /> <br /> Want to write for Exalted? Look at the <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/submission-guidelines/\" target=\"_blank\">freelancer submission guidelines</a>.","e